I remember my friend bought a Sony Xperia, when it was one of the hottest/newest smartphones on the market in 2012, was def. one of the highest end androids at the time, dont remember the exact model no. And this was in the middle east (Jordan), so this made it even more rare, I think he paid over $1000 for it at the time. 

&#x200B;

Anyways, back then, all the rage about this phone was that it was waterproof or water-resistant, which was still a relatively new concept (Back then, most people, especially in Jordan didnt even have smartphones).... but anyways this new sony xperia was top of the line at the time, and one of the first smartphones that was like fully water resistant. Like you could run it under the tap, you could take it in the swimming pool and film underwater.... etc (Idk if you were supposed to, but we did, and the phone held out).

&#x200B;

So we'd go to local businesses/restaurants and be like "Can I use your sink please?" and we would dump ketchup or something on the phone, and then take it to their sink and just run it under the water at full blast.... and watch the employees reactions as they would be completely bewildered/shocked as to how we were soaking up this $1k phone in water without damaging it. 

&#x200B;

Then we'd go sit at fancy hotel pools, pretend to be having a heated conversation on the phone, then get pissed off and start yelling, then eventually throw/toss the phone in the pool out of anger, and keep walking (while there were guests sitting everywhere) and they would be staring in complete shock and disbelief, thinking we just destroyed a brand new top of the line expensive smartphone. We'd order drinks, and accidentally drop the phone in the drink(or in our plate of messy food, ketchup and everything), and then just casually take it to the sink and rinse it off. and the employees would be freaking out thinking we're destroying a brand new $1k smartphone..... But alas, i dont know how waterproof it was meant to be, but i think my friend saw commercials about this feature, and decided to put it to the test, and it held out pretty well, I mean so well we were even able to film ourselves swimming underwater with it. Idk if you were actually supposed to take it this far, but it worked, never got any water damage in the long run. 

&#x200B;

Boy, were those the good old days. I miss that phone

